consiSt("hi",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Hawai‘i","d":"Court of last resort. Reviews Intermediate Court of Appeals decisions only by discretionary writ of certiorari, and takes cases directly from the ICA docket by transfer (mandatory for questions of imperative or fundamental public importance, rulings invalidating a statute or constitutional amendment, and life-without-parole sentences; discretionary for novel questions or conflicting decisions). Admits and disciplines lawyers and approves pro hac vice practice rules."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Intermediate Court of Appeals (ICA)","d":"Every appeal allowed by law from a court or agency goes first to the ICA, subject to transfer to the Supreme Court or later certiorari review. Decides cases in panels; a motions panel handles defaults and motions before a case is submitted on the merits."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Circuit Courts","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts organized by judicial circuit, hearing civil actions (including all jury trials), felonies, and probate, trust, and guardianship matters. Civil practice is governed by the Hawai‘i Rules of Civil Procedure plus the Rules of the Circuit Courts. The Family Court is a division of the circuit court."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"District Courts (including Small Claims Division)","d":"Civil cases up to $40,000 (exclusive up to $10,000, not counting fees, interest, and costs), summary possession, and specific performance up to $20,000. Bench trials only: a timely jury demand transfers the case to circuit court. District courts cannot hear defamation, libel, slander, malicious prosecution, false imprisonment, or cases where title to real estate is in question."},{"l":"SPECIALIZED","n":"Land Court and Tax Appeal Court","d":"Separate courts with their own rules; final judgments of the land court are appealed to the ICA like circuit court judgments."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Hawaii Public Expression Protection Act (UPEPA)","d":"Hawai‘i adopted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act as HRS chapter 634G (L 2022, c 96). The older anti-SLAPP chapter, HRS ch. 634F (Citizen Participation in Government), was repealed by the same act. The effective date of Act 96 is not shown in the collected text, so check it for claims based on conduct around 2022.","c":"HRS ch. 634G; HRS ch. 634F (repealed, L 2022, c 96, §3)","v":true},{"t":"Scope: three kinds of protected activity, three exclusions","d":"Covers claims based on (1) communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (2) communication on an issue under consideration in such a proceeding; or (3) exercise of free speech, press, assembly, petition, or association rights under the U.S. or Hawai‘i Constitution on a matter of public concern. Excluded: claims against a governmental unit or its employee acting officially; government enforcement actions against an imminent threat to public health or safety; and claims against a business arising from a communication about selling or leasing its goods or services (dramatic, literary, musical, political, journalistic, and artistic works are not ‘goods or services’). The official text misprints the section heading as ‘§643G-2’.","c":"HRS § 634G-2","v":true},{"t":"Special motion to dismiss: 60 days from service, and everything stops","d":"File no later than 60 days after service of the pleading asserting the claim, or later for good cause, notwithstanding court rules. Filing automatically stays all other proceedings between the moving and responding parties, including discovery and pending motions; the court may also stay related proceedings involving other parties. The stay lasts until the order and the time to appeal it expire. Limited discovery is allowed only if specific information is needed to meet the dismissal burden and is not otherwise reasonably available. A fee motion, voluntary dismissal, severance, and good-cause rulings on unrelated motions or public-safety injunctions are not stayed.","c":"HRS § 634G-3","v":true},{"t":"Hearing within 60 days; ruling within 60 days after hearing","d":"The court must hear the motion no later than 60 days after filing, unless it orders later for allowed discovery (then within 60 days after the discovery order) or other good cause, and must rule no later than 60 days after the hearing. The court considers the pleadings, motion papers, and any evidence admissible on summary judgment under the Hawai‘i Rules of Civil Procedure.","c":"HRS §§ 634G-4, 634G-5, 634G-7","v":true},{"t":"Three-step test; dismissal is with prejudice","d":"The court must dismiss with prejudice if the movant shows the chapter applies, the responding party fails to show an exclusion, and either the responding party fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact. A voluntary dismissal without prejudice does not defeat the movant’s right to a ruling and fees; a voluntary dismissal with prejudice means the movant prevailed for fee purposes.","c":"HRS § 634G-6","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory fees for a winning movant","d":"The court ‘shall award’ costs, reasonable attorney’s fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a prevailing movant. A prevailing responding party gets them only if the court finds the motion frivolous or filed solely to delay.","c":"HRS § 634G-9","v":true},{"t":"Immediate appeal of a denial, 30 days, with a full stay","d":"A moving party may appeal as of right within 30 days from an order denying the motion in whole or in part. While that appeal is pending, all proceedings between all parties are stayed. The Act must be construed liberally to protect speech, press, assembly, petition, and association rights, with attention to uniformity among enacting states.","c":"HRS §§ 634G-3(d), 634G-8, 634G-10, 634G-11","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: discretionary, through local counsel only","d":"A lawyer actively licensed by the highest court of any U.S. state, territory, or D.C. may associate with a Hawai‘i bar member (local counsel) in a specific case, at the discretion of the presiding judge. Local counsel must file the petition or motion in the court where the case is pending and send a copy to the Office of Disciplinary Counsel. Admission carries over to an appeal or remand in the same case without a new motion.","c":"RSCH Rule 1.9(a), (a)(1), (b)","v":true},{"t":"What the papers must show, including Hawai‘i tax registration","d":"Local counsel’s declaration must state an address for service, that local counsel is attorney of record responsible for all phases of the case, a DCCA certificate of good standing for local counsel’s employer dated within 10 days (or an exemption explanation), and a Hawai‘i general excise tax license number with a promise to pay state income and GET (or why that does not apply). The applicant’s declaration must list every admission and good standing, any discipline (with details of any pending matter), every Hawai‘i case where the applicant sought or got pro hac vice status and its status, the same DCCA certificate and GET license items, a promise to follow Hawai‘i law, the HRPC, and the Guidelines of Professional Courtesy and Civility, and designate local counsel as agent for disciplinary service.","c":"RSCH Rule 1.9(b)(1)–(2)","v":true},{"t":"Repeat appearances need ‘special circumstances’","d":"If the applicant has made more than one appearance as counsel in Hawai‘i in the preceding five years, the declaration must explain the special circumstances that justify another admission.","c":"RSCH Rule 1.9(b)(2)(E)","v":true},{"t":"Annual fees: pay within 10 days or the order dies","d":"For each year the order is in effect, the attorney pays the Hawai‘i State Bar an annual Disciplinary Board fee and an annual Lawyers’ Fund for Client Protection fee (one set of fees covers all cases), plus any Bar registration fee. Proof of payment must be filed in the case within 10 days after the order and within 10 days after each December payment. Missing the first 10-day deadline voids the order (a new motion is needed); missing a December payment automatically suspends the right to appear until arrears and a reinstatement fee are paid. The dollar amounts are not stated in the rule.","c":"RSCH Rule 1.9(a)(2)–(3)","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel signs everything and must be ready to try the case","d":"Local counsel must sign all pleadings, motions, briefs, and other documents, participate actively in all phases, and be prepared to go forward. Service on local counsel is service on pro hac vice counsel and the client. Local counsel must send the order to the Bar and ODC and notify them when the engagement ends, the case closes, or the order lapses.","c":"RSCH Rule 1.9(d)–(e)","v":true},{"t":"Pro hac vice counsel cannot e-file","d":"Attorneys must register for and file through the Judiciary Electronic Filing System (JEFS), but a lawyer admitted pro hac vice may not register as a JEFS user, because all filings must be made by local counsel.","c":"HEFSR Rule 4.1(a); HRCP Rule 1.1; HRAP Rule 25(a)","v":true},{"t":"Arbitration in Hawai‘i also needs approval","d":"An out-of-state lawyer representing a party in a Hawai‘i arbitration over a Hawai‘i-related dispute must associate with a Hawai‘i lawyer and get written approval from the arbitrator(s) on a petition served on all parties, with declarations similar to court pro hac vice (including that the lawyer is not a Hawai‘i resident and not regularly practicing there). The same annual fees and 10-day rule apply. Collective-bargaining arbitrations and agencies with their own rules are excluded. A later court proceeding requires a separate Rule 1.9 admission.","c":"RSCH Rule 1.9A","v":true},{"t":"Unauthorized practice is a statutory offense","d":"Appearing without pro hac vice approval exposes the lawyer to Supreme Court discipline and referral for violating HRS § 605-14, which makes it unlawful to practice law unless licensed or authorized by a court, agency, or statute.","c":"RSCH Rules 1.9(f), 1.9A(f); HRS § 605-14","v":true},{"t":"No admission on motion in the collected rules","d":"Admission to the Hawai‘i bar requires passing the Hawai‘i Bar Examination (held in Honolulu) and the MPRE; the collected rules contain no admission-by-motion path. A lawyer who did not attend an ABA-accredited school may sit for the exam after actively practicing five of the last six years in another U.S. jurisdiction. Limited admissions exist for military judge advocates, legal-services lawyers, and uniformed-services spouses. A 2025 bar-application ‘pilot project’ is linked on the rules page but was not collected.","c":"RSCH Rules 1.3(b)(2), 1.3(g), 1.7, 1.16, 1.17","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances are formalized","d":"A lawyer giving limited-scope help may file a Notice of Limited Appearance (Form 32-A) and, once the agreed work is done, a Notice of Withdrawal (Form 32-B); the client has 14 days to object. Withdrawing early, or without having filed the notice, requires a Rule 25.1 motion. A ghostwritten filing need not name the lawyer but must state on its first page: “This document was prepared with the assistance of an attorney.”","c":"HRCP Rule 11.1; HRPC Rule 1.2(c)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: older Model Rules language","d":"The Hawai‘i Rules of Professional Conduct (Exhibit A to the Supreme Court rules) follow the ABA Model Rules’ numbering but often keep pre-2002 wording, such as ‘consent after consultation’ instead of ‘informed consent’ (for example in Rules 1.2(c), 1.6(a), 1.7(b)(4), 1.8). The latest amendments took effect January 1, 2026. Pro hac vice applicants must also promise to follow the Guidelines of Professional Courtesy and Civility for Hawai‘i Lawyers.","c":"HRPC Rules 1.2, 1.6, 1.7, 1.8; RSCH Rule 1.9(b)(2)(F)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: no ‘non-refundable’ fees; written terms required","d":"The scope and basis of the fee must be communicated in writing (except for regular clients on the same basis, or total cost of $250 or less). Advance fees are presumed unearned and go into trust, and no agreement may call a fee non-refundable or earned on receipt. A flat fee over $1,500 requires a signed written agreement listing six items, including milestones or an hourly rate for earning the fee and a refund of any unearned portion. Contingent fees need a signed writing that also warns about expenses owed even if the client loses.","c":"HRPC Rule 1.5(b)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: mandatory disclosure of client fraud that used your services","d":"Beyond the ABA permissive exceptions, Hawai‘i requires (‘shall reveal’) disclosure of information that clearly establishes a client’s criminal or fraudulent act, done using the lawyer’s services, that caused substantial financial or property injury, to the extent needed to rectify it. Government lawyers may also disclose to prevent or rectify criminal or illegal acts by public officials or agencies harmful to the public good.","c":"HRPC Rule 1.6(b)(5)–(6), (c)","v":true},{"t":"Conflicts: older 1.8 provisions","d":"There is no ABA-style exception for modest humanitarian gifts to indigent litigants; a lawyer may only advance costs or pay them for an indigent client. A lawyer related to opposing counsel as parent, child, sibling, spouse, or domestic partner needs the client’s consent after consultation. Aggregate settlements need each client’s signed written consent.","c":"HRPC Rule 1.8(e), (g), (k)","v":true},{"t":"Lateral screening is narrower than the ABA rule","d":"A lateral lawyer’s conflict from a former firm’s matter is not imputed only if the lawyer did not participate in the matter and has no confidential information about it, is timely screened and gets no part of the fee, and written notice goes to the former client.","c":"HRPC Rule 1.10(c)","v":true},{"t":"Intermediary rule retained","d":"Hawai‘i keeps Rule 2.2 (lawyer as intermediary between clients), which the ABA deleted. Conflicts under Rule 2.2 are imputed to the whole firm.","c":"HRPC Rules 2.2, 1.10(a)","v":true},{"t":"No multijurisdictional-practice safe harbor","d":"Hawai‘i Rule 5.5 has no counterpart to ABA Rule 5.5(c)–(d): there is no rule-based permission for temporary practice by out-of-state lawyers or for in-house counsel. It instead bars letting a suspended or disbarred person in the office have contact with clients.","c":"HRPC Rule 5.5; HRS § 605-14","v":true},{"t":"Misconduct: no ‘prejudicial to justice’ catch-all; sexual harassment rule","d":"Rule 8.4(d) is ‘Reserved’, so there is no ABA-style catch-all for conduct prejudicial to the administration of justice. Hawai‘i adds failure to cooperate with an ethics investigation (8.4(g)) and bars sexual harassment in a professional capacity (8.4(h)), but has no general anti-discrimination rule like ABA 8.4(g).","c":"HRPC Rule 8.4","v":true},{"t":"Trust accounts, solicitation, and pro bono","d":"Client funds must be held in a separate account in Hawai‘i, with records kept for 6 years after the representation ends. Written solicitation about a personal-injury or wrongful-death claim is barred until more than 30 days after the injury or death. Pro bono goal is 50 hours a year, or a $500 contribution to the Hawai‘i Justice Foundation or a legal-services provider instead. Rules 7.4 and 7.5 (fields of practice, firm names) are retained.","c":"HRPC Rules 1.15(a), 7.3(e)(1), 6.1, 7.4, 7.5","v":true}],"style":"State of Hawai‘i","home":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Answer in 20 days, not 21","d":"An answer is due 20 days after service of the summons and complaint. After a Rule 12 motion is denied, the answer is due in 10 days. There is no waiver-of-service procedure that extends the deadline.","c":"HRCP Rules 4, 12(a)"},{"t":"Post-trial motions are due in 10 days","d":"Renewed judgment-as-a-matter-of-law, amended-findings, and new-trial/alter-or-amend motions must be filed no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28). The court cannot extend these deadlines.","c":"HRCP Rules 6(b), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)"},{"t":"The 90-day deemed-denial trap on tolling motions","d":"A timely post-judgment tolling motion extends the appeal time, but if the court does not enter an order within 90 days the motion is denied by operation of the rule. Under the current rule, the appeal clock runs from the clerk’s notice of that denial. From January 1, 2027, with no filed written order within 90 days, the notice of appeal is due 120 days after the motion was filed, and a Rule 60(b) motion tolls only if filed within 30 days of judgment.","c":"HRAP Rule 4(a)(3) (current; amended eff. Jan. 1, 2027)"},{"t":"Motion practice runs on 18/8/3 days and page limits","d":"Hearing motions must be filed and served at least 18 days before the hearing, oppositions at least 8 days, and replies at least 3 days. Mail service adds 2 days, not 3. Memoranda are capped at 20 pages (replies 10), and memoranda over 15 pages need tables of contents and authorities.","c":"HRCP Rule 6(d)–(e); RCCH Rules 7(b), 7.1, 8"},{"t":"Dispositive motions die 50 days before trial","d":"Motions seeking judgment or dismissal of any claim, including summary judgment, must be filed no later than 50 days before the assigned trial date unless the court allows otherwise for good cause.","c":"RCCH Rule 7(f); HRCP Rule 56(b)"},{"t":"Serve within 6 months or risk dismissal","d":"HRCP has no 90-day service limit, but the court may dismiss any action or claim on its own, with written notice, if no service is made within 6 months after filing. A motion to reinstate must be filed within 10 days of the dismissal order.","c":"RCCH Rule 28"},{"t":"Jury demand within 10 days","d":"Serve a written jury demand no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (federal: 14) and file it, or the jury right is waived. In district court, a jury demand sends the case to circuit court.","c":"HRCP Rule 38(b), (d); HRS § 604-5(b)"},{"t":"Don’t file defamation in district court","d":"District courts lack jurisdiction over libel, slander, defamation, malicious prosecution, false imprisonment, and real-property title disputes, whatever the amount.","c":"HRS § 604-5(d)"},{"t":"No temporary-practice safe harbor and no e-filing account","d":"Hawai‘i Rule 5.5 has no ABA 5.5(c) temporary-practice exception, so out-of-state lawyers need pro hac vice even for limited court work. Pro hac vice counsel cannot register for JEFS, and local counsel must sign and file everything.","c":"HRPC Rule 5.5; RSCH Rule 1.9(d); HEFSR Rule 4.1(a)"},{"t":"Pay the pro hac vice fees within 10 days","d":"If the annual Disciplinary Board and Lawyers’ Fund fees are not paid, with proof filed, within 10 days after the order granting pro hac vice, the order is no longer valid and a new motion must be filed. Unpaid December renewals bring automatic suspension.","c":"RSCH Rule 1.9(a)(2)–(3)"},{"t":"‘Non-refundable’ retainers are forbidden","d":"No fee agreement may describe a fee as non-refundable or earned on receipt. Advance fees are presumed unearned and must be held in trust, and flat fees over $1,500 need a signed agreement with refund terms.","c":"HRPC Rule 1.5(b)–(c)"},{"t":"Appellate briefs are limited by pages, with strict points of error","d":"Opening and answering briefs are capped at 35 pages and replies at 10, in 12-point type. Each point of error must say where in the record the error occurred and where it was objected to (quoting the evidence, instruction, or finding at issue); nonconforming points are disregarded. Hawai‘i cases since statehood must be cited to both the state and regional reporters.","c":"HRAP Rules 28(a), 28(b)(1), (4), 32(b)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory challenge of a judge","d":"The collected rules and statutes give no right to remove a trial judge without cause. Disqualification requires a reason.","c":"HRS § 601-7; HRCP (no provision)","v":true},{"t":"Affidavit of bias: one per case, before the hearing","d":"A party may file an affidavit stating facts and reasons showing the judge’s personal bias or prejudice for or against a party. It must be filed before the trial or hearing (or good cause shown for lateness), only one affidavit is allowed per party per case, and it must come with counsel’s certificate of good faith. A judge is also disqualified if a relative within the third degree is counsel or a party, the judge has a more than de minimis financial interest (waivable after full disclosure), or the judge was counsel or ruled below. Case notes on the official site say the statute is strictly construed and does not apply to appellate justices.","c":"HRS § 601-7(a)–(b)","v":true},{"t":"Appellate recusal motion: 10 days","d":"A motion to disqualify or recuse an ICA judge or Supreme Court justice is due within 10 days after the appellate case is opened or after new information is discovered, or the objection is waived absent good cause. It must rest on Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11 grounds, be supported by a declaration of facts, and certify good faith; disagreement with past rulings is not enough.","c":"HRAP Rule 5","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Anti-SLAPP protects Hawai‘i constitutional rights too","d":"Chapter 634G covers speech, press, assembly, petition, and association rights ‘guaranteed by the United States Constitution or the Hawaii State Constitution’ and must be construed liberally to protect them.","c":"HRS §§ 634G-2(a)(3), 634G-10","v":true},{"t":"Hawai‘i Constitution speech provisions not collected","d":"Whether the Hawai‘i Constitution’s speech guarantees are read more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked against collected text.","c":"n/a","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Two-step trial setting with hard pretrial cutoffs","d":"Within 14 days after any defendant is served or appears, the plaintiff must file a notice requesting a scheduling conference; failure can lead to dismissal. The judge then issues a scheduling order within the earlier of 90 days after service or 60 days after appearance. By default discovery closes 60 days before trial; plaintiffs must name all witnesses 60 days before the discovery cutoff and defendants 30 days before it.","c":"RCCH Rule 12(a)(2), (a)(4), (d), (i), (j); HRCP Rule 16","v":true},{"t":"Expedited track by agreement","d":"By agreement, cases can go on an expedited track to trial within 9 months of the scheduling conference, with 4 depositions (16 hours total) and 35 combined written discovery requests. Other cases are set for trial within 12 months (up to 18 on request).","c":"HRCP Rule 16.1","v":true},{"t":"Unpublished appellate decisions can be cited","d":"Dispositions filed on or after July 1, 2008 may be cited for persuasive value. A copy of any cited unpublished disposition must be attached to the brief.","c":"HRAP Rule 35(c)(2) (amended July 21, 2026)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Hawai‘i Rules of Appellate Procedure (official Judiciary PDF, pages Release 06/25–06/26; index effective date July 1, 2026), plus orders amending HRAP Rule 50 (eff. July 14, 2026), Rule 35 (eff. July 21, 2026), and Rule 4 (filed July 15, 2026, eff. January 1, 2027); Hawai‘i Rules of Civil Procedure (Release 06/26); Rules of the Circuit Courts (Release 12/25); Hawai‘i Electronic Filing and Service Rules; HRS §§ 601-7, 602-57 to 602-59, 641-1, and ch. 634G (legislature data site). Summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Hawai‘i: everything starts at the ICA, and finality is strict","d":"Almost every civil appeal goes first to the Intermediate Court of Appeals (ICA). The Supreme Court takes cases from the ICA by transfer before decision, or reviews ICA decisions by discretionary certiorari. The notice of appeal is due 30 days after entry of an appealable judgment or order, and no appellate court can enlarge the Rule 4 deadlines. Two Hawai‘i features catch newcomers. The 10-day post-judgment motions have a 90-day deemed-denial rule, which changes on January 1, 2027. And Hawai‘i requires a separate written judgment that resolves all claims, or certification for appeal, which the statement of jurisdiction must prove claim by claim."},"groups":[["Which court and what is appealable",[{"t":"ICA hears all appeals first","d":"Appeals are allowed from all final judgments, orders, and decrees of the circuit, district, and land courts to the ICA, which also issues writs in aid of its jurisdiction. The Supreme Court reaches the case by transfer or certiorari.","c":"HRS §§ 641-1(a), 602-57"},{"t":"Separate, complete judgment","d":"Entry means filing in the clerk’s office, and every judgment must be set out in a separate document. With multiple claims or parties, a judgment on fewer than all is final only with an express determination that there is no just reason for delay and an express direction for entry. In circuit court the prevailing party must prepare the proposed judgment within 10 days after the decision; objections to form are due within 5 days.","c":"HRCP Rules 54(b), 58; RCCH Rule 23(a)–(b); HRAP Rule 4(a)(5)"},{"t":"Prove jurisdiction early","d":"Within 10 days after the record on appeal is filed, each appellant and cross-appellant must file a statement of jurisdiction (10 pages maximum). In multi-claim or multi-party cases it must show, with record cites, how each claim against each party was resolved or that the judgment was certified, with the order attached. Failure can lead to dismissal.","c":"HRAP Rule 12.1"},{"t":"Interlocutory appeals need the circuit court’s permission","d":"A circuit court may allow an appeal from an order denying a motion to dismiss or any interlocutory order if it would speed the end of the litigation, and its refusal cannot be reviewed. The motion for leave is due within 30 days after entry of the order; if granted, the notice of appeal is due within 30 days after the order granting leave.","c":"HRS § 641-1(b); HRAP Rule 4(a)(1)"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP denials are immediately appealable","d":"A party whose special motion to dismiss under the Hawaii Public Expression Protection Act is denied in whole or part may appeal as of right within 30 days, and all proceedings are stayed during the appeal.","c":"HRS §§ 634G-8, 634G-3(d)"},{"t":"Extraordinary writs go to the Supreme Court","d":"Petitions for mandamus or prohibition directed to a judge are filed with the appellate clerk, served on the judge, all parties, and the attorney general, and submitted to the Supreme Court to decide whether to entertain them.","c":"HRAP Rule 21(a)"}]],["Notice of appeal and tolling",[{"t":"30 days; file in the appellate court through JEFS","d":"The civil notice of appeal is due within 30 days after entry of the judgment or appealable order. Attorneys who are JEFS users file it electronically with the appellate court; self-represented and exempt filers file it with the clerk of the court appealed from. A mailed notice is timely if postmarked in time and received within 5 days. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed right after entry. Attach the judgment or order, and file the Civil Appeal Docketing Statement with the notice.","c":"HRAP Rules 3(a), 3(c)(2), 3.1(b), 4(a)(1)–(2)"},{"t":"Serve the notice and file proof within 7 days","d":"The appellant serves a filed copy of the notice on every other party and files proof of service with the appellate court within 7 days. Missing any step other than the timely notice does not void the appeal but may bring sanctions, including dismissal.","c":"HRAP Rule 3(a), (e)(1)"},{"t":"Tolling motions and the 90-day rule (current)","d":"A timely motion for judgment as a matter of law, amended findings, new trial, reconsideration or alter/amend, or attorney’s fees or costs extends the appeal time for all parties until 30 days after the order deciding it. The court must decide within 90 days. If it does not, within 5 days after the 90th day the clerk notifies the parties that the motion is denied and later orders are void, and the appeal time runs from that notice. The notice of appeal covers all timely post-judgment motions.","c":"HRAP Rule 4(a)(3) (in force through Dec. 31, 2026)"},{"t":"Tolling rule from January 1, 2027","d":"The amended rule lists the tolling motions by rule: HRCP 50(b), 52(b), 59; Rule 60(b) only if filed within 30 days of judgment; probate Rule 36(b) within 30 days; HRCP 54 fee or cost motions only if filed within 14 days; and agency motions. A filed written order within 90 days starts a new 30-day period. With no filed written order within 90 days, the motion is denied, later orders are void, and the notice of appeal is due 120 days after the motion was filed. No clerk’s notice is needed.","c":"HRAP Rule 4(a)(3) (amended July 15, 2026, eff. Jan. 1, 2027)"},{"t":"Extensions only from the trial court, capped at 30 days","d":"The court or agency appealed from may extend the time for good cause on a motion filed within the original period, or for excusable neglect on a motion filed within 30 days after it expires. No extension may exceed 30 days past the original deadline. No court may change Rule 4’s jurisdictional requirements, and default in filing a timely notice of appeal cannot be excused.","c":"HRAP Rules 4(a)(4), 26(b), 26(e)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals","d":"Any party may cross-appeal within 14 days after the notice of appeal is served on it, or within the time for the notice of appeal, whichever is later. The cross-appeal survives dismissal of the main appeal, and separate opening and answering briefs are filed on it.","c":"HRAP Rules 4.1(b)(1), 4.1(d), 28(h)"}]],["Record, briefing, and form",[{"t":"Transcripts and findings within 10 days","d":"Within 10 days after filing the notice of appeal, request the transcripts needed, or certify that none are needed. If the trial court must enter findings once an appeal is filed, request them in that court within 10 days, and the judge must enter them within 28 days. The clerk below files the record within 60 days after the notice of appeal.","c":"HRAP Rules 10(b)(1)(A), 10(f), 11(b)(1)"},{"t":"Briefing schedule","d":"Opening brief: 40 days after the record on appeal is filed. Answering brief: 40 days after service of the opening brief, or after docketing, whichever is later. Reply: 14 days after service of the answering brief; if none will be filed, notify the clerk before the deadline. Mail service adds 2 days.","c":"HRAP Rules 28(b)–(d), 26(c)"},{"t":"Extensions of briefing time","d":"The appellate clerk grants one extension on a timely request (received within the original time): up to 30 days for an opening or answering brief, 10 days for a reply. Further extensions require a motion filed at least 5 days before the due date and good cause. A pending request does not toll the deadline.","c":"HRAP Rule 29"},{"t":"Page limits, not word limits","d":"Opening and answering briefs are limited to 35 pages and replies to 10 pages, excluding indexes, appendices, and the statement of related cases. Use 12-point type (Times New Roman, Courier New, or Arial qualify), double or 1.5 spacing, and 1-inch margins. Footnotes must be in the same size, and condensing print is prohibited.","c":"HRAP Rules 28(a), 32(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Opening brief contents are enforced","d":"The opening brief must include numbered points of error stating the error, where it occurred in the record, and where it was objected to, with required quotations for evidence, instruction, and finding points. It also needs a separate standard-of-review section, record cites by JEFS/JIMS docket number and electronic page, the challenged rulings appended, and a statement of related cases. Points not presented this way are disregarded, and points not argued may be deemed waived.","c":"HRAP Rule 28(b)(1)–(11)"},{"t":"Late or nonconforming briefs","d":"An appeal may be dismissed, or a brief stricken, for late or nonconforming briefs. An appellee’s default may lead the court to accept the appellant’s statement of facts as true. Once a brief is filed, the court must give notice and a chance to cure first.","c":"HRAP Rule 30"},{"t":"Citing unpublished decisions","d":"Memorandum opinions and unpublished orders filed on or after July 1, 2008 may be cited for persuasive value, with a copy appended. Earlier ones generally may not be cited.","c":"HRAP Rule 35(c) (amended July 21, 2026)"},{"t":"Appellate mediation","d":"If an appeal is selected for the Hawai‘i Appellate Mediation Program, participation is required, and transcript, record, jurisdiction-statement, and briefing deadlines are stayed until the clerk returns the case to the docket.","c":"HRAP Rules 3.1(e), 33(a)"}]],["Stays and costs",[{"t":"Stay: ask the trial court first","d":"Stays, supersedeas bonds, and injunctions pending appeal are normally sought first in the court appealed from. An appellate motion must show that asking below was impracticable, or was denied with reasons. Execution is automatically stayed for only 10 days after entry of judgment, and a supersedeas bond stays enforcement once approved; the State and counties need no bond.","c":"HRAP Rule 8(a); HRCP Rule 62(a), (d)–(e)"},{"t":"Fees and costs on appeal","d":"Request fees and costs with an itemized, verified bill (Form 8 for non-indigent requests) and a statement of authority no later than 14 days after the reconsideration period expires or reconsideration is decided. Objections are due in 10 days and replies in 7. Copying costs are capped at 20¢ per page.","c":"HRAP Rule 39(c)–(d)"}]],["Rehearing and Supreme Court review",[{"t":"Reconsideration: 10 days, one motion","d":"A motion for reconsideration is due within 10 days after the opinion, order, or ruling. It must state with particularity what was overlooked or misapprehended and include a good-faith declaration. No response unless requested. If the court does not act within 10 days, the motion is rejected, and only one motion is allowed per party.","c":"HRAP Rule 40"},{"t":"Certiorari: 30 days plus an automatic 30","d":"Apply for a writ of certiorari within 30 days after the ICA’s judgment on appeal or dismissal order. A written request filed within that 30 days gets an automatic 30-day extension; an untimely request extends nothing. The application is limited to 20 pages, a response (optional) to 20 pages within 15 days (automatic 15-day extension on timely request), and a reply to 5 pages within 7 days. Grounds are grave errors of law or fact or obvious inconsistencies. The Court must accept within 60 days or the application is rejected, and there is no reconsideration.","c":"HRAP Rule 40.1 (eff. April 28, 2026); HRS § 602-59"},{"t":"Transfer to the Supreme Court before ICA decision","d":"Any party may apply to transfer a case from the ICA no earlier than 10 days after the record is filed and no later than 20 days after the last brief is or could have been filed. The explanation is limited to 10 pages, and responses are due in 10 days. Transfer is mandatory for questions of imperative or fundamental public importance, rulings invalidating a constitutional amendment or holding a statute, ordinance, or rule invalid, and life-without-parole sentences. It is discretionary for novel questions or conflicting decisions. The Court decides within 45 days, deadlines cannot be extended, and the application does not stay other deadlines.","c":"HRAP Rule 40.2 (amended eff. July 1, 2026); HRS § 602-58"},{"t":"When the ICA judgment takes effect","d":"The ICA judgment becomes effective on the 31st day after entry if no certiorari application is filed (or when an extension expires). A timely application stays it until the Supreme Court rejects, dismisses, or affirms. Reconsideration, transfer, or certiorari is not required to exhaust state remedies.","c":"HRAP Rules 36(c), 41, 40.3"}]],["E-filing and counsel",[{"t":"JEFS is mandatory for attorneys","d":"Unless excused, every attorney appearing in the appellate courts must register as a JEFS user and file everything through JEFS. Filing is complete when the Notice of Electronic Filing issues, and the NEF time stamp controls. Each PDF document and exhibit is limited to 10 MB. Pro hac vice counsel cannot register, so local counsel files.","c":"HRAP Rule 25(a); HEFSR Rules 2.2, 2.3, 3.1, 3.3, 4.1(a)"},{"t":"Withdrawal and substitution of appellate counsel","d":"Withdrawal requires a motion served on the client, but a client-signed notice of withdrawal and substitution works without a motion. Since July 14, 2026, a firm may also file a notice of disaffiliation or staffing change naming continuing counsel and showing the client was informed.","c":"HRAP Rule 50 (amended July 14, 2026)"},{"t":"Recusal of appellate judges: 10 days","d":"A motion to disqualify an appellate judge or justice is due within 10 days after the appellate case is opened or after newly discovered information, or the objection is waived absent good cause.","c":"HRAP Rule 5(b)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil)","d":"30 days from entry (filing) of the judgment or appealable order.\nJurisdictional. Mailed notice timely if postmarked in time and received within 5 days. File the Civil Appeal Docketing Statement with it.","c":"HRAP 4(a)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry)","note":"Jurisdictional. Mailed notice timely if postmarked in time and received within 5 days. File the Civil Appeal Docketing Statement with it."}},{"t":"Proof of service of notice of appeal","d":"7 days from filing of the notice of appeal.","c":"HRAP 3(e)(1)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion for leave for interlocutory appeal (circuit court)","d":"30 days from entry of the interlocutory order.\nIf granted, the notice of appeal is due 30 days after entry of the order granting leave.","c":"HRAP 4(a)(1); HRS § 641-1(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the interlocutory order)","note":"If granted, the notice of appeal is due 30 days after entry of the order granting leave."}},{"t":"Renewed JMOL / amend findings / new trial / alter or amend","d":"10 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended (HRCP 6(b)). A timely motion tolls the appeal time.","c":"HRCP 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended (HRCP 6(b)). A timely motion tolls the appeal time."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal after a tolling motion","d":"30 days from entry of the order deciding the motion (or the clerk’s deemed-denial notice).\nThe court must rule within 90 days of the motion; otherwise it is deemed denied. From Jan. 1, 2027: 30 days after a filed written order entered within 90 days, otherwise 120 days after the motion was filed.","c":"HRAP 4(a)(3) (current)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order deciding the motion)","note":"The court must rule within 90 days of the motion; otherwise it is deemed denied. From Jan. 1, 2027: 30 days after a filed written order entered within 90 days, otherwise 120 days after the motion was filed."}},{"t":"Extension of time for notice of appeal","d":"30 days from expiration of the original appeal period.\nMotion in the court appealed from: good cause if filed before expiry, excusable neglect if filed within 30 days after. Extension capped at 30 days past the original deadline.","c":"HRAP 4(a)(4)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the original appeal period)","note":"Motion in the court appealed from: good cause if filed before expiry, excusable neglect if filed within 30 days after. Extension capped at 30 days past the original deadline."}},{"t":"Cross-appeal","d":"14 days from service of the notice of appeal on the cross-appellant.\nOr within the time for the notice of appeal, whichever is later.","c":"HRAP 4.1(b)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the notice of appeal on the cross-appellant)","note":"Or within the time for the notice of appeal, whichever is later."}},{"t":"Transcript request (or certificate that none needed)","d":"10 days from filing of the notice of appeal.","c":"HRAP 10(b)(1)(A)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Request for findings of fact/conclusions of law (where required after appeal)","d":"10 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nThe judge must enter them within 28 days of the request.","c":"HRAP 10(f)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"The judge must enter them within 28 days of the request."}},{"t":"Record on appeal filed by clerk below","d":"60 days from filing of the notice of appeal.","c":"HRAP 11(b)(1)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Statement of jurisdiction","d":"10 days from filing of the record on appeal.\n10-page limit. Appellees contesting jurisdiction file within the same period.","c":"HRAP 12.1(a)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the record on appeal)","note":"10-page limit. Appellees contesting jurisdiction file within the same period."}},{"t":"Opening brief","d":"40 days from filing of the record on appeal.\n35-page limit. One clerk extension of up to 30 days on a timely request (HRAP 29(a)).","c":"HRAP 28(b)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the record on appeal)","note":"35-page limit. One clerk extension of up to 30 days on a timely request (HRAP 29(a))."}},{"t":"Answering brief","d":"40 days from service of the opening brief (or docketing, if later).\n35-page limit. One clerk extension of up to 30 days.","c":"HRAP 28(c)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (service of the opening brief)","note":"35-page limit. One clerk extension of up to 30 days."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"14 days from service of the answering brief.\n10-page limit. One clerk extension of up to 10 days. If not filing, notify the clerk before the deadline.","c":"HRAP 28(d)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the answering brief)","note":"10-page limit. One clerk extension of up to 10 days. If not filing, notify the clerk before the deadline."}},{"t":"Motion for further brief extension","d":"5 days from before the brief’s due date (at least).\nGood cause required. Does not toll the deadline.","c":"HRAP 29(b)","due":{"n":5,"from":"the trigger date (before the brief’s due date)","note":"Good cause required. Does not toll the deadline."}},{"t":"Motion to disqualify appellate judge/justice","d":"10 days from opening of the appellate proceeding or discovery of new information.\nOtherwise waived absent good cause.","c":"HRAP 5(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (opening of the appellate proceeding or discovery of new information)","note":"Otherwise waived absent good cause."}},{"t":"Motion for reconsideration (appellate)","d":"10 days from filing of the opinion, dispositional order, or ruling.\nIf the court does not act within 10 days, the motion is rejected. One motion per party.","c":"HRAP 40(a)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the opinion, dispositional order, or ruling)","note":"If the court does not act within 10 days, the motion is rejected. One motion per party."}},{"t":"Request for appellate fees and costs","d":"14 days from expiration of the reconsideration period or decision on reconsideration.\nObjections due in 10 days, replies in 7 (HRAP 39(d)(4)).","c":"HRAP 39(d)(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the reconsideration period or decision on reconsideration)","note":"Objections due in 10 days, replies in 7 (HRAP 39(d)(4))."}},{"t":"Application for writ of certiorari","d":"30 days from filing of the ICA judgment on appeal or dismissal order.\nA written request filed within the 30 days gets an automatic 30-day extension. 20-page limit.","c":"HRAP 40.1(a)(1); HRS § 602-59(c)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the ICA judgment on appeal or dismissal order)","note":"A written request filed within the 30 days gets an automatic 30-day extension. 20-page limit."}},{"t":"Response to certiorari application","d":"15 days from filing of the application.\nOptional. Automatic 15-day extension on timely request. Reply within 7 days, 5 pages.","c":"HRAP 40.1(e)(1)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the application)","note":"Optional. Automatic 15-day extension on timely request. Reply within 7 days, 5 pages."}},{"t":"Supreme Court action on certiorari","d":"60 days from filing of the application.\nNo acceptance within 60 days = rejection.","c":"HRAP 40.1(g)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the application)","note":"No acceptance within 60 days = rejection."}},{"t":"Application for transfer to Supreme Court (latest)","d":"20 days from filing (or due date) of the last brief.\nNot earlier than 10 days after the record is filed. Response 10 days. Decision within 45 days. No extensions.","c":"HRAP 40.2(a)(2)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (filing)","note":"Not earlier than 10 days after the record is filed. Response 10 days. Decision within 45 days. No extensions."}},{"t":"Appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP motion","d":"30 days from order denying the special motion to dismiss.\nAs of right. All proceedings are stayed pending appeal (HRS § 634G-3(d)).","c":"HRS § 634G-8","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (order denying the special motion to dismiss)","note":"As of right. All proceedings are stayed pending appeal (HRS § 634G-3(d))."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.courts.state.hi.us/"]]},"rulesName":"Hawai‘i Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope, interpretation, e-filing, automation","s":"The rules govern all civil suits, at law or in equity, in the circuit courts (exceptions in Rule 81) and are construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action. They are read together with the Hawai‘i Electronic Filing and Service Rules (HEFSR), the Hawai‘i Court Records Rules, and the Rules of the Circuit Courts; documents filed and notices given under the HEFSR satisfy every filing, mailing, notice, and service requirement in these rules, and duties may be performed by automation.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"1.1","t":"Registration required (JEFS)","s":"Unless exempted by the court, every attorney representing a party in a case kept in the Judiciary Information Management System (JIMS) must register as a Judiciary Electronic Filing and Service System (JEFS) user and file all documents electronically. A self-represented individual party may register as a JEFS user for that party’s own case. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the “civil action.”","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement of action","s":"A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint with the court.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"3.1","t":"Civil information sheet; additional claims; transfers from district court","s":"Every initial complaint must be filed with a fully completed civil information sheet (Form 2-A), and every later affirmative pleading with an additional claims information sheet (Form 2-B). In a case transferred from district court, the plaintiff must file the civil information sheet within 7 days after the notice of docketing is filed. No federal counterpart (compare the federal civil cover sheet, which is a local requirement).","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"4","t":"Process","s":"The clerk issues the summons when the complaint is filed; it must warn of default and bar personal delivery between 10:00 p.m. and 6:00 a.m. on premises not open to the public unless a judge permits it in writing on the summons. Service may be made anywhere in the State by the sheriff or deputy, a court-appointed person, any nonparty at least 18 years old, or (within a county) the police chief or authorized subordinate; the rule lists methods for individuals, infants (also serve the infant if 16 or older), incompetents, entities, the State (the attorney general or a deputy), State officers and agencies, and counties (corporation counsel or county attorney). Publication under HRS §§ 634-23, 634-26, and 634-36 omits the caption and follows Form 1-A; proof of service is due within the time the person served has to respond. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 4, the text contains no waiver-of-service procedure and no 90-day time limit for service.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Every pleading after the complaint, discovery paper, written motion (other than ex parte), notice, brief, offer of judgment, and similar paper must be served on every party, but parties in default for failure to appear need be served only with new or additional claims (served like a summons). Service on a represented party goes to the attorney, by delivery, mail to the last known address, or leaving it with the clerk if no address is known; service by mail is complete on mailing and service through JEFS or JIMS on transmission of the Notice of Electronic Filing. Papers served conventionally on a non-JEFS user are filed with a certificate of service before or within a reasonable time after service; discovery papers are not filed unless offered at a motion or trial or ordered filed, and the clerk may not reject a paper only for improper form.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"Computation excludes the trigger day and includes the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, or holiday (holidays are those designated under HRS § 8-1); for periods under 7 days, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded. The court may enlarge time for cause before expiration, or after expiration for excusable neglect, but not the times under Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (d), (e), 60(b), or HRAP 4(a) except as those rules allow. A written motion and notice of hearing must be served at least 18 days before the hearing and opposing affidavits at least 8 days before (unless the rules or an order say otherwise; Rule 59(c) differs), and 2 days are added after service by mail. The 18-day motion notice and 2-day mail addition differ from the federal 14-day and 3-day periods; the rule was last amended May 21, 2026, effective July 1, 2026.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions","s":"The allowed pleadings are a complaint and answer, a reply to a counterclaim denominated as such, an answer to a cross-claim, a third-party complaint and third-party answer; the court may order a reply to an answer. Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state the grounds with particularity and the relief sought, and be signed under Rule 11. Demurrers, pleas, and exceptions for insufficiency are abolished.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"A claim needs a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment; alternative or different types of relief may be demanded. Denials must fairly meet the substance of the averments, lack of knowledge has the effect of a denial, and the listed affirmative defenses (accord and satisfaction through waiver, including injury by fellow servant) must be pleaded. Averments other than damages are admitted if not denied in a required responsive pleading; alternative and inconsistent claims or defenses are allowed subject to Rule 11.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity need not be pleaded, but a challenge to capacity or legal existence must be made by specific negative averment. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity, while malice, intent, and knowledge may be averred generally; denial of conditions precedent must be specific and particular. Averments of time and place are material, and special damages must be specifically stated.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"The caption names the court, title of the action, file number, and Rule 7(a) designation; the complaint names all parties. The first page must have either a 3-inch top margin or a 3-inch-by-3-inch space in the top right corner for an electronic file stamp, a requirement not in the federal rule. Averments go in numbered paragraphs, and an exhibit to a pleading is part of it for all purposes.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing; representations to the court; sanctions","s":"Every paper must be signed by at least one attorney of record (or by the self-represented party) and list address and telephone number; JEFS or JIMS filings are signed under HEFSR Rule 5, and documents an attorney prepares for an unrepresented party must comply with Rule 11.1(c). Presenting a paper certifies proper purpose, legal warrant, and evidentiary support after reasonable inquiry. A sanctions motion must be made separately and may not be filed unless the challenged paper is not withdrawn or corrected within 21 days after service (safe harbor); monetary sanctions are not available against a represented party for legal contentions or on the court’s own initiative after voluntary dismissal or settlement, and the rule does not apply to discovery.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"11.1","t":"Limited appearance and withdrawal","s":"An attorney giving limited-scope help under HRPC Rule 1.2 may file a “Notice of Limited Appearance” (Form 32-A) and may submit in camera an “Agreement and Consent to Limited Representation” (Form 32); filing outside the agreed scope extends the notice. After finishing the agreed services the attorney may file a Notice of Withdrawal of Limited Appearance; the client may object within 14 days, which triggers a hearing, and earlier withdrawal (or withdrawal without a notice of limited appearance) requires a Rule 25.1 motion. A ghostwritten paper need not name the attorney but must state on its first page “This document was prepared with the assistance of an attorney” (not required for nonprofit, court, or government program help), and the drafting attorney is deemed to make the Rule 11 certifications. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections; motion for judgment on the pleadings","s":"An answer is due 20 days after service of the summons and complaint (not 21 as in the federal rule), and an answer to a cross-claim or reply to a counterclaim is due within 20 days; after a Rule 12 motion is denied or deferred, or a more definite statement is served, the responsive pleading is due within 10 days. The seven Rule 12(b) defenses may be raised by pre-answer motion, and a 12(b)(6) or 12(c) motion that relies on outside matter becomes a Rule 56 motion. A more definite statement must be furnished within 10 days after notice of the order; a motion to strike is due before responding or within 20 days after service; personal jurisdiction, venue, process, and service defenses are waived if omitted from a Rule 12 motion or the first responsive pleading, and subject-matter jurisdiction may be raised any time. The text has no counterpart to the federal 60-day answer period for defendants who waive service or for the United States.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and cross-claim","s":"A counterclaim arising from the same transaction or occurrence is compulsory unless it is already the subject of another pending action or the opposing party sued by attachment without personal jurisdiction; other counterclaims are permissive and may exceed or differ from the opposing claim. The rules do not enlarge the right to counterclaim against the State or a county. Counterclaims that mature later may be added by supplemental pleading with leave, omitted counterclaims by amendment with leave, cross-claims against co-parties may arise from the same transaction or property, and additional parties may be joined under Rules 19 and 20.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may serve a third-party complaint on a nonparty who is or may be liable to it for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim without leave if it files the third-party complaint within 10 days after serving its original answer (federal practice allows 14); otherwise leave on motion with notice to all parties is required. The third-party defendant answers under Rules 12 and 13, may assert the third-party plaintiff’s defenses and its own claims against the plaintiff, and the plaintiff may assert related claims against the third-party defendant. A plaintiff facing a counterclaim may implead on the same basis; the text has no admiralty provision.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after service if no responsive pleading is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar; otherwise only with leave (freely given) or written consent. A motion or stipulation to amend must attach the full proposed amended pleading in Ramseyer format (additions underscored, deletions bracketed and stricken) without incorporating the prior pleading by reference, and the granted pleading is filed with Ramseyer formatting removed; response is due within the time left on the original or 10 days after service, whichever is longer. Amendments to conform to evidence and relation back follow the older federal model; the relation-back text for a changed party contains no time limit tied to service of process, and supplemental pleadings are allowed on motion.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","s":"Under the version effective January 1, 2022, within the earlier of 14 days after any defendant is served or appears, the plaintiff must file a notice requesting a scheduling conference (Form 33) and serve the resulting order or notice; lead counsel or the party must attend. Unless the case is exempt under the Rules of the Circuit Courts, the court must issue a scheduling order within the earlier of 90 days after any defendant is served or 60 days after any defendant appears; the order must set the trial date, limit time for joinder, amendment, discovery, and motions, and assign or decline to assign the case to the Rule 16.1 expedited track. A schedule may be modified only for good cause with the judge’s consent, the final pretrial order only to prevent manifest injustice, and noncompliance draws Rule 37(b)(2)(B)–(D) sanctions and mandatory expenses. The PDF still prints the version in effect before January 1, 2022 alongside the current one.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"16.1","t":"Expedited or non-expedited track; discovery limits; trial setting","s":"By agreement of the parties, the court may assign a non-exempt case to an expedited track through the scheduling order for cases that can be resolved within 9 months of the scheduling conference; foreclosure, Court Annexed Arbitration Program cases (HRS § 601-20), agency appeals, consumer debt collection, quiet title, and asbestos cases are exempt. Expedited-track parties are limited to 4 oral depositions totaling 16 hours on the record and 35 combined interrogatories (with subparts), document requests, and requests for admission; more requires a motion or streamlined request under RCCH Rule 15.1 with a good-faith conferral declaration, or a court-approved stipulation. Expedited cases are set for trial within 9 months of the initial scheduling conference; other covered cases within 12 months, or on request up to 18 months; continuances need good cause. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"16.2","t":"Appearance by telephone or videoconference","s":"Absent good cause, the court must allow parties or counsel to appear remotely for scheduling and trial-setting conferences, status conferences, uncontested motions, and other approved matters, and may require in-person appearance midway. Remote appearance is presumptively not allowed for trials, evidentiary hearings, contested or dispositive motions, and settlement conferences unless permitted. A party granted remote appearance must notify all others at least 48 hours before the hearing, and the first party to get permission arranges the call. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","s":"Actions must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, with reasonable time allowed after objection for ratification, joinder, or substitution; subdivision (b) is reserved (the text has no capacity-by-domicile provision like the federal rule). A guardian may sue or defend for an infant or incompetent, who otherwise sues by next friend or guardian ad litem. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 17(d) authorizes suing an unidentified (fictitious-name) defendant if the pleading describes the person and the diligent efforts made to identify them; subject to HRS § 657-22 the person is then treated as a party with notice, the plaintiff must identify the defendant by motion for certification before filing its pretrial statement (or later as allowed), and a defendant substantially prejudiced by delay may obtain dismissal.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"A party may join as many claims, legal or equitable, as it has against an opposing party. Claims ordinarily cognizable only after another is concluded may be joined, so a plaintiff may seek money and set aside a fraudulent conveyance without first obtaining a money judgment.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"19","t":"Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","s":"A person subject to service must be joined if complete relief is otherwise impossible or the person’s interest would be impaired or would expose parties to inconsistent obligations; a refusing plaintiff may be made a defendant or involuntary plaintiff. If joinder is not feasible, the court weighs four equity-and-good-conscience factors to decide whether to proceed or dismiss. Pleadings must name nonjoined needed persons and the reasons; the text omits the federal venue-objection language.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact. Judgment may be given for or against fewer than all, and the court may order separate trials or other orders to prevent embarrassment, delay, or expense.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be dropped or added by court order at any stage on just terms, and any claim may be severed.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"Claimants against the plaintiff may be joined and required to interplead when the plaintiff is or may be exposed to double or multiple liability, even if their claims lack a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability; a defendant may obtain interpleader by cross-claim or counterclaim. The text has no reference to statutory interpleader.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"Class certification requires numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy plus one of the three Rule 23(b) categories, decided by order as soon as practicable; (b)(3) classes get the best practicable notice and a right to opt out by a specified date. A class action may not be dismissed or compromised without court approval and notice to all members. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 23, the text has no interlocutory-appeal subdivision (23(f)) or class-counsel and fee subdivisions (23(g)–(h)); instead Rule 23(f) requires the court to determine each member’s amount before a (b)(3) judgment or settlement approval, take a report of amounts actually paid, and order distribution of any unpaid residue, which the court may direct to nonprofits eligible for the indigent legal assistance fund under HRS § 607-5.7 or to the Hawai‘i Justice Foundation.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Derivative actions by shareholders","s":"A shareholder or member suing to enforce a right of a corporation or unincorporated association must file a verified complaint alleging contemporaneous ownership (or devolution by operation of law) and, with particularity, the demand made on the directors and shareholders or members and why it failed or was not made. The action fails if the plaintiff does not fairly and adequately represent similarly situated holders, and it may not be dismissed or compromised without court approval and notice. The text omits the federal allegation that the action is not collusive to confer jurisdiction.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"23.2","t":"Actions relating to unincorporated associations","s":"An action by or against association members as a class through named representatives may proceed only if the representatives will fairly and adequately protect the association’s and members’ interests; Rule 23(d) orders and Rule 23(e) dismissal and compromise procedures apply. The rule does not bar actions by or against an association under a statute.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"On timely application, intervention is of right when a statute confers an unconditional right or the applicant’s interest may be impaired and is not adequately represented, and permissive when a statute confers a conditional right or a common question exists; State or county officers and agencies may intervene when a party relies on a law or regulation they administer. The motion must be served under Rule 5 with a pleading setting out the claim or defense. A party challenging a statute’s constitutionality in a case where the State or its agency, officer, or employee is not a party must give immediate written notice to the State Attorney General (Rule 24(d)), which differs from the federal notice-and-certification procedure in Fed. R. Civ. P. 5.1.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"If a party dies and the claim survives, a motion to substitute (served on parties under Rule 5 and on nonparties like a summons) must be made within 120 days after death is suggested on the record by a served statement, or the action is dismissed as to the deceased party; the federal period is 90 days. Substitution is also available for incompetency and transfer of interest, and a public officer’s successor is automatically substituted.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"25.1","t":"Withdrawal, substitution, and appearance of counsel","s":"Except as RCCH Rule 10(c) provides, withdrawal or substitution of counsel in circuit court is effective only with court approval, subject to HRPC Rule 1.16. A withdrawal-and-substitution must cite authority, bear the signatures of the withdrawing and substituting attorneys and the client, show the trial date, and include an “APPROVED AND SO ORDERED” signature line; a motion to withdraw must be served on the client, cite authority, state that the client was told in writing of its RCCH Rule 4 duties and that an entity may appear only through Hawai‘i-admitted counsel, and give the client’s last known address and telephone. An attorney not already appearing through the initial pleading, an answer, or a substitution must immediately file a notice of appearance with name, Hawai‘i bar number, address, telephone, and party represented. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"26","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Under the version effective January 1, 2022, parties must make initial disclosures (trial witnesses other than experts, supporting documents, damages computations, and insurance declarations pages and any reservation-of-rights letters) at or within 14 days after the Rule 26(f) conference, or within 30 days after being served or joined later; foreclosure, Court Annexed Arbitration Program, agency appeal, consumer debt, quiet title, and asbestos cases are exempt, and no discovery may be sought before the Rule 26(f) conference except in those exempt cases or by rule, stipulation, or order. Unless otherwise ordered, expert disclosures (with signed reports from retained experts listing publications for 10 years and testimony for the prior 3 years) are due 120 days before trial for the party with the burden of proof, 90 days before trial for the opposing party, and 60 days before trial for rebuttal; draft reports and most attorney-expert communications are protected, and an expert who must give a report may be deposed only after it is provided. The scope of discovery remains any nonprivileged matter relevant to the subject matter and “reasonably calculated to lead to” admissible evidence, without the federal proportionality definition; the parties must confer at least 21 days before the scheduling conference and file a written discovery plan within 14 days after conferring, protective-order motions need a good-faith conferral certification, objections to a discovery-conference motion are due within 10 days, and discovery papers must be signed under Rule 26(h). The PDF still prints the pre-2022 version alongside the current one.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"A person expecting to be a party to an action cognizable in a Hawai‘i court may file a verified petition to perpetuate testimony in the circuit court of the circuit where any expected adverse party resides, with notice served at least 20 days before the hearing in the manner of Rule 4(d); the court appoints an attorney for unserved adverse parties. While an appeal is pending or the time to appeal has not expired, the trial court may allow depositions to perpetuate testimony on motion. Perpetuated depositions may be used in later Hawai‘i actions on the same subject under Rule 32(a).","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"In the United States, depositions are taken before an officer authorized by Hawai‘i, federal, or local law, or a person appointed by the court; abroad, under a treaty or convention, a letter of request, on notice before an authorized person, or before a commissioned person. A deposition may not be taken before a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or of counsel, or anyone financially interested in the action.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","s":"Unless the court directs otherwise, the parties may stipulate in writing to deposition logistics and to modify other discovery procedures or limits. Two exceptions: the expedited-track limits in Rule 16.1(c) may be changed only as that rule provides, and stipulations extending the response times under Rules 33, 34, and 36 need court approval if they would interfere with any time set for completing discovery, hearing a motion, or trial. The PDF prints two substantively identical versions (before and effective January 1, 2022).","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"Any party may depose any person after the action begins; leave is needed for a plaintiff’s deposition within 30 days after service on any defendant (unless the defendant has sought discovery or the notice certifies the deponent is about to leave the State or the United States or go to sea), for a prisoner, for more than 10 depositions per side under Rules 30 and 31, or to re-depose someone, absent written stipulation. The notice must state the recording method (the noticing party pays), may include a Rule 34 request to a party, may name an organization under Rule 30(b)(6), and must tell the deponent in substantially the prescribed words of the right to request review; if review is requested before the deposition ends, the deponent has 30 days after notice that the transcript is available to review and sign a statement of changes, and failure to give the notice bars use of the transcript until the 30-day review is allowed. A deposition is limited to 1 day of 7 hours unless stipulated or ordered, objections must be concise and non-suggestive, instructions not to answer are limited to privilege, court limits, or a Rule 30(d)(4) motion, remote depositions require stipulation or order, and depositions may be destroyed 6 months after final disposition including appeal.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions upon written questions","s":"A party may depose any person on written questions without leave except for a prisoner, more than 10 depositions per side, or a repeat deposition. Cross questions are due within 14 days after the notice and questions are served, redirect within 7 days after cross, and recross within 7 days after redirect; the court may enlarge or shorten these times. The officer takes the answers under Rule 30(c), (e), and (f), and the noticing party gives prompt notice of filing.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"A deposition may be used against a party present, represented, or given reasonable notice: for impeachment or as the Hawai‘i Rules of Evidence permit, by an adverse party for any purpose if the deponent was a party or its officer, director, managing agent, or designee, and by any party if the witness is dead, unable to attend, could not be subpoenaed, exceptional circumstances exist, or—unlike the federal 100-mile test—the witness resides on an island other than the place of trial or hearing or is out of the State. Under Rule 32(b), which has no federal counterpart in this rule, deposition designations are due at least 30 days before trial, objections within 14 days afterward, and undisclosed objections other than under Hawai‘i Rules of Evidence 402 and 403 are waived. A deposition may not be used against a party that, after less than 11 days’ notice, promptly moved for a protective order still pending at the deposition; objections to the form of written questions must be served within the time for the next round and within 5 days after the last questions.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Without leave or stipulation, a party may serve up to 60 interrogatories, counting subparts as separate questions (the federal limit is 25). Answers and objections are due within 30 days after service, except that a defendant may respond within 45 days after service of the summons and complaint; objections must be specific or are waived absent good cause. Contention interrogatories are allowed, and business records may be specified where the burden of deriving the answer is substantially the same.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"34","t":"Production of documents, electronically stored information, and things; entry on land","s":"A party may request inspection, copying, testing, or sampling of designated documents, ESI, and tangible things in the responding party’s possession, custody, or control, or entry on land. The written response is due within 30 days after service, or for a defendant within 45 days after service of the summons and complaint, and must state objections with reasons and permit inspection of the rest. Documents are produced as kept in the usual course or organized by category; ESI is produced in the form requested or, absent a request, as ordinarily maintained or reasonably usable, and only once absent good cause. Nonparties are reached by Rule 45 subpoena.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examination of persons","s":"When a party’s (or a person in its custody or control’s) mental or physical condition, including blood group, is in controversy, the court may order an examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner on motion for good cause and notice, specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. On request, the party causing the examination must provide the detailed report and earlier reports of the same condition and may then get reciprocal reports; requesting the report or deposing the examiner waives privilege as to other examiners of the same condition.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"A party may request admission of facts, opinions of fact, application of law to fact, and genuineness of documents (with copies attached). A matter is admitted unless a signed written answer or objection is served within 30 days after service, but a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons and complaint unless the court shortens the time; lack of knowledge is no excuse without reasonable inquiry. Admissions are conclusive for the pending action only and may be withdrawn or amended on motion if the merits are served and the requesting party is not prejudiced. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 36, the text sets no numerical limit, though Rule 26(b)(2) lets the court limit the number of requests.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to make or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","s":"A motion to compel (filed in the court where the action is pending, or for a deposition in the circuit where it is taken; for a nonparty, in the circuit of the deposition) must include a good-faith conferral certification; evasive or incomplete answers count as failures, and expenses including attorney’s fees follow the result unless the position was substantially justified. Disobeying a discovery order (including a Rule 26(f) order) can lead to established facts, preclusion, striking pleadings, dismissal, default, contempt, and mandatory expenses; failing to supplement under Rule 26(e)(2) bars use of the undisclosed witness or information unless harmless, and costs of proving matters not admitted under Rule 36 are shifted absent listed exceptions. Hawai‘i-specific provisions: expenses and fees may not be awarded against the State or a county except as a statute permits (Rule 37(e)), and absent exceptional circumstances no sanction may be imposed for ESI lost through routine, good-faith operation of an electronic system (Rule 37(f)), which is not the current federal Rule 37(e) framework.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"The constitutional and statutory jury right is preserved. A party must serve a written jury demand no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (the federal period is 14 days) and file it under Rule 5(d); another party may demand jury trial of other issues within 10 days after service of a partial demand. On a statutory de novo appeal to circuit court, the jury demand goes in the notice of appeal or, for the appellee, within 10 days after docketing; failure to serve and file a demand waives the jury, and a demand may not be withdrawn without consent of the parties.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate in writing or on the record to a bench trial or the court finds no jury right exists. Undemanded issues are tried to the court, though the court may order a jury on motion; an advisory jury or a consent jury with binding effect is available in nonjury cases.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"40","t":"Assignment of cases for trial","s":"The circuit courts provide by rule for placing actions on the trial calendar, with or without a party’s request, giving precedence to actions entitled to it by statute.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"A plaintiff may dismiss without order by notice filed before the return date under Rule 12(a) or before service of an answer or summary judgment motion, whichever comes first, or by stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared, in the form required by Rule 41.1; dismissal is without prejudice unless stated, but a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise dismissal at the plaintiff’s request requires a court order, and not over the objection of a defendant with a pending counterclaim that cannot stand alone. Unlike the federal rule, the court may dismiss sua sponte for failure to prosecute or to comply with the rules or an order on written notice, and the dismissal may be set aside for good cause on motion filed no later than 10 days after the dismissal order; involuntary dismissals other than for jurisdiction, venue, or Rule 19 operate as adjudications on the merits unless the order says otherwise.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"41.1","t":"Documents dismissing an action or part of it","s":"Notices and stipulations of dismissal under Rules 41(a)(1)(A), 41(a)(1)(B), and 41(c) must be presented before filing to the assigned judge (including in Court-Annexed Arbitration Program cases) or, if no judge is assigned, to the Legal Documents Branch/Section. Each must show below the title the judge (or “none”) and trial date (or “none”), cite the specific Rule 41 subsections, and for a Rule 41(a)(1)(A) notice state whether the dismissed party served an answer or summary judgment motion. A dismissal of the whole case is titled “Stipulation for [or Notice of] Dismissal With [or Without] Prejudice of All Claims and Parties” and states “all other claims and parties are dismissed”; a partial dismissal identifies the dismissed and remaining parties and claims, and counsel identify the parties they represent. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Actions with a common question of law or fact may be joined for hearing or trial or consolidated. The court may order separate trials of claims or issues for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for expedition and economy, preserving the jury right.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"43","t":"Taking of testimony","s":"Trial testimony is taken orally in open court unless the Hawai‘i Rules of Evidence, a statute, or other Hawai‘i Supreme Court rules provide otherwise; the text does not include the federal provision on remote testimony by contemporaneous transmission. The court may schedule all expert testimony during the same phase of trial; offers of proof may be made when an objection is sustained, affirmation may replace oath, motions may be heard on affidavits, and the court may appoint and fix pay for interpreters, taxable as costs.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"44","t":"Proof of official record","s":"A domestic official record may be proved by official publication or a copy attested by the custodian with a certificate of custody from a judge of a court of record or a sealed public officer; foreign records require attestation and a final certification by a U.S. or foreign diplomatic or consular official, which the court may excuse for good cause or a treaty may make unnecessary. A certified statement of diligent search proves the absence of a record, and other lawful methods of proof remain available.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Determination of foreign law","s":"A party intending to raise foreign law must give notice by pleading or other reasonable written notice. The court may consider any relevant material or source, whether or not admissible under the Hawai‘i Rules of Evidence, and its determination is a ruling on a question of law.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"The clerk of the circuit court where the action is pending issues subpoenas under seal, signed but otherwise blank, for the requesting party to complete; a subpoena may require production of documents, ESI, or things, and the court may quash or modify an unreasonable and oppressive subpoena on motion made by the compliance time or condition denial on advance payment of production costs. Subpoenas may be served anywhere in the State by the sheriff or a deputy, any nonparty at least 18, or (within a county) the police, by delivery with tender of one day’s fees and mileage (not required for the State or a county). For depositions, proof of service of the deposition notice authorizes the clerk of the circuit where the deposition is taken to issue subpoenas; written objection to inspection is due within 10 days after service or by the compliance time if sooner, after which inspection requires a court order. A resident may be required to attend only in the county of residence, employment, or in-person business, and a nonresident served in the State only in the county of service, unless the court fixes another convenient place. The text follows the pre-1991 federal model and lacks the federal 100-mile limits and Rule 45(d)(1) undue-burden duty.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"46","t":"Exceptions unnecessary","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; it is enough to make known the desired action or objection and its grounds when the ruling is made or sought, and a party with no opportunity to object is not prejudiced.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"At the court’s discretion each party may give a brief “mini-opening statement” to the panel before jury selection, and the court must permit the parties or their attorneys to examine prospective jurors (or to supplement the court’s examination), unlike the federal rule, which leaves attorney voir dire to the court’s discretion. Up to 6 alternate jurors may be seated, with 1 extra peremptory per side for 1–2 alternates, 2 for 3–4, and 3 for 5–6, usable only against alternates. Jurors may submit written questions for witnesses at the court’s discretion after review with counsel outside the jury’s hearing, may take notes unless the court articulates good cause on the record, and may be excused for good cause.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"48","t":"Juries of less than twelve; majority verdict","s":"The parties may stipulate to a jury of fewer than 12 or to a verdict or finding by a stated majority (see HRS § 635-20). Unlike the federal rule, the text does not set a minimum of 6 jurors or require unanimity absent stipulation.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"49","t":"Special verdicts and interrogatories","s":"The court may require a special verdict with written findings on each fact issue; an omitted issue is waived unless a party demands its submission before the jury retires, and otherwise the court may find it or is deemed to have found consistently with the judgment. With a general verdict and interrogatories, harmonious answers yield judgment under Rule 58, consistent answers inconsistent with the verdict may support judgment on the answers, further deliberation, or a new trial, and inconsistent answers bar judgment.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"50","t":"Judgment as a matter of law; new trial; conditional rulings","s":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law may be made any time before submission to the jury and must specify the judgment sought and its legal and factual basis. A renewed motion must be filed no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (the federal period is 28 days), alone or with a Rule 59 new-trial request, and the court may let the judgment stand, order a new trial, or direct judgment. If the renewed motion is granted, the court rules conditionally on any new-trial motion, and a party against whom judgment as a matter of law is rendered must file any Rule 59 motion within 10 days after entry of judgment.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"51","t":"Instructions to jury","s":"The court may pre-instruct the jury on elements and defenses before evidence; parties file written requests at the close of evidence or earlier as directed, and the court marks each “given,” “refused,” or modified, with unobjected instructions marked “given by agreement” and not later challengeable. The court may revise and combine instructions in writing after hearing counsel, may not orally qualify, modify, or explain instructions, and must give written supplemental instructions after showing them to counsel. Except for good cause articulated on the record, the court instructs before closing arguments and gives each juror, including alternates, a written copy; error is preserved only by objection before the jury retires stating the matter and grounds. These procedures differ substantially from Fed. R. Civ. P. 51, which has no marking, written-only, or plain-error provisions of this kind.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the court","s":"In bench trials and with advisory juries, and when ruling on interlocutory injunctions, the court must find facts specially and state conclusions of law separately; findings are reviewed for clear error, and findings are unnecessary on Rule 12 or 56 motions. A motion to amend findings or the judgment must be made no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (the federal period is 28 days) and may be joined with a Rule 59 motion. The court may enter judgment on partial findings after a party is fully heard, supported by findings and conclusions.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"The court may appoint a special master (including a referee, auditor, examiner, or assessor), fix compensation charged to the parties or a fund, and the master may get execution against a party that fails to pay. Reference is the exception: in jury cases only when issues are complicated, and in bench cases only for accounts, difficult damages computations, or an exceptional condition. The order of reference may limit powers and fix the time for the report; the text is far shorter than the modern federal Rule 53.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments; costs; attorneys’ fees","s":"“Judgment” includes a decree and any appealable order; with multiple claims or parties, the court may enter final judgment on fewer than all only upon an express determination of no just reason for delay and an express direction for entry. Default judgments may not differ in kind or exceed the demand, but other judgments grant all relief to which the party is entitled. Costs go to the prevailing party as of course (against the State or a county only as law permits) and may be taxed by the clerk on 48 hours’ notice, with court review on motion served within 5 days; attorneys’ fee motions must be filed and served no later than 14 days after entry of an appealable order or judgment unless a statute or order provides otherwise.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"55","t":"Default","s":"The clerk enters default when a party fails to plead or otherwise defend. The clerk may enter judgment for a sum certain on affidavit against a defendant defaulted for failure to appear who is not an infant or incompetent; otherwise the court enters judgment, and a party that has appeared must receive written notice of the application at least 3 days before the hearing (the federal period is 7 days). Default may be set aside for good cause and a default judgment under Rule 60(b); no default judgment may be entered against the State or a county, or their officers or agencies, unless the claimant proves the claim by evidence satisfactory to the court.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A claimant may move after 20 days from commencement (or after the adverse party moves), and a defending party at any time, but any summary judgment motion must be filed and served no less than 50 days before trial unless the court permits otherwise for good cause. The motion must be filed and served at least 18 days before the hearing, opposition at least 8 days before, and any reply at least 3 days before; judgment is rendered if there is no genuine issue of material fact, and an interlocutory judgment on liability alone is allowed. The rule keeps the pre-2010 federal structure (affidavits on personal knowledge, no resting on pleadings, Rule 56(f) continuance for unavailable facts, expenses and possible contempt for bad-faith affidavits) and adds Rule 56(h): an order disposing of fewer than all claims must state each claim decided and the parties for and against whom it was decided.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Declaratory judgment procedure under statute follows these rules, with jury rights under Rules 38 and 39; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, except that declaratory relief is not available in any controversy about taxes. The court may order a speedy hearing and advance the case.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry of judgment","s":"Unless the court directs otherwise, and subject to Rule 54 and RCCH Rule 23, the prevailing party prepares and submits the proposed judgment. Filing the judgment in the clerk’s office is its entry, and it is not effective before entry; entry is not delayed for taxing costs, and every judgment must be on a separate document. The text has no counterpart to the federal rule deeming judgment entered 150 days after docketing.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"59","t":"New trials; altering or amending judgment","s":"A new trial may be granted on the grounds historically recognized in Hawai‘i actions at law (jury cases) or for rehearing in equity (bench cases). A new-trial motion, and any motion to alter or amend the judgment, must be filed no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (the federal period is 28 days); opposing affidavits are due 10 days after service, extendable up to 20 days by the court for good cause or written stipulation. The court may order a new trial on its own within 10 days after entry of judgment, or grant a timely motion on unstated grounds after notice, and must specify the grounds.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected any time, but once an appeal is docketed only with leave of the appellate court. Relief from a final judgment, order, or proceeding is available for the six familiar reasons (mistake or excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, fraud, voidness, satisfaction or changed prospective equity, any other reason); the motion must be made within a reasonable time and, for the first three reasons, not more than one year after entry. A Rule 60(b) motion does not affect finality or suspend operation; independent actions and fraud-on-the-court relief remain, and the old writs are abolished.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"No error in admitting or excluding evidence or in any ruling is a ground for a new trial or for disturbing a judgment unless refusing relief would be inconsistent with substantial justice; the court must disregard errors that do not affect substantial rights.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"No execution or enforcement may issue until 10 days after entry of judgment (the federal automatic stay is 30 days), but judgments in injunction or receivership actions and accounting orders are not stayed unless the court orders otherwise. The court may stay enforcement pending Rule 50, 52(b), 59, or 60 motions on conditions securing the adverse party, may modify or grant injunctions pending appeal, and the appellant obtains a stay on appeal by a supersedeas bond, effective when the court approves the bond. No bond is required when the State or a county or its officer or agency appeals; the appellate courts’ power to stay is preserved, and Rule 54(b) judgments may be stayed pending later judgments.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"63","t":"Inability of a judge to proceed","s":"If a judge cannot continue a commenced trial or hearing, another judge may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record and finding no prejudice. In a nonjury trial or hearing, the successor must, on request, recall any available witness whose testimony is material and disputed, and may recall others.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizure of person or property","s":"All Hawai‘i-law remedies for seizing a person or property to secure satisfaction of the eventual judgment are available at the start of and during an action, including arrest, attachment, garnishment, replevin, and sequestration.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions","s":"No preliminary injunction issues without notice, and the court may consolidate the hearing with trial on the merits while preserving jury rights. An ex parte temporary restraining order requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable injury and the attorney’s written certification of notice efforts; it must state the date and hour, define the injury, and expire within the time the court sets, not to exceed 10 days (the federal limit is 14), extendable for a like period for good cause or longer by consent, and the adverse party may move to dissolve it on 2 days’ notice. Security is discretionary (“may require”), not mandatory as under the federal rule, and is never required of the State or a county or their officers or agencies; orders must state reasons and describe the restrained acts specifically, and the rule does not modify HRS § 128-29 (civil defense and emergency cases).","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"65.1","t":"Security: proceedings against sureties","s":"A surety on a bond or undertaking given under these rules submits to the court’s jurisdiction and irrevocably appoints the clerk as its agent for service. Liability may be enforced by motion without an independent action, with the motion served on the clerk, who mails copies to sureties with known addresses.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers appointed by courts","s":"An action in which a receiver has been appointed may not be dismissed except by court order. Administration of estates by receivers follows prior practice; otherwise these rules govern actions seeking a receiver or brought by or against one.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit in court","s":"In an action seeking money or another deliverable thing, a party may, with notice to every other party and leave of court, deposit all or part of it with the court; deposit and withdrawal follow court orders.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of settlement or judgment","s":"More than 10 days before trial begins, any party (not only a defending party, as in the federal rule) may serve an offer of settlement or an offer to allow judgment against either party, with costs then accrued. Acceptance must be served within 10 days after service of the offer, after which the clerk enters dismissal or judgment; if the offeree’s final judgment is not more favorable than the offer, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer. After liability is determined, an offer served within a reasonable time not less than 10 days before the damages hearing has the same effect.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution","s":"Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution unless the court directs otherwise, with procedure under State law. The judgment creditor, or a successor in interest of record, may obtain discovery from any person, including the judgment debtor, by deposition procedures.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"If a party fails to perform an act required by judgment (such as conveying land or delivering documents), the court may have another appointed person do it at the party’s cost with the same effect, the clerk issues writs of attachment or sequestration on application, and contempt is available. For property within the State the court may enter a judgment divesting and vesting title, and a party awarded possession gets a writ of execution or assistance from the clerk.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"71","t":"Process in behalf of and against persons not parties","s":"A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it by the same process as a party, and a nonparty against whom an order may lawfully be enforced is subject to the same process as a party.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"72","t":"Appeal to a circuit court","s":"Where a statute allows circuit-court review of a decision of a non-court governmental official or body, the appeal is by notice of appeal filed in the circuit court within 30 days after the appellant is notified of the decision as the statute provides; a mailed notice is timely if postmarked within the period and received within 5 days after the postmark, and a certified copy must be served promptly on each appellee. Within the notice period (or up to 30 more days for good cause), the appellant files a designation of record, an Order for Certification and Transmission of the Record (agency must transmit within 20 days, issued through JEFS for JEFS users), and a statement of the case treated as a complaint; appellees may counter-designate within 10 days. Opening brief is due 40 days after the record is filed, answering brief 40 days after service of the opening brief, and an optional reply within 14 days (or written notice that none will be filed), subject to HRAP Rule 28(a) page limits; jury trial requires a Rule 38 demand, unpaid costs or bonds require dismissal, and filing does not stay the agency action unless a statute or the court for good cause provides otherwise. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"73–76","t":"Deleted","s":"Rules 73 through 76 are deleted.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"77","t":"Circuit courts and clerks","s":"Circuit courts are always open for filing, and documents filed through JEFS or JIMS are deemed filed with the clerk; trials are in open court, while other proceedings may be in chambers anywhere, but no non-ex parte hearing may be held outside the circuit without all affected parties’ consent. The clerk’s office is open business hours except weekends and holidays and may grant process, defaults, and default judgments not requiring court order. On entry of a judgment or an order requiring notice of entry, the clerk mails notice only to unrepresented parties not in default who are not JEFS users or have not consented to electronic service, the presenting party must serve a copy under Rule 5, electronic filing is notice to registered JEFS users, and lack of notice does not extend appeal time except under HRAP Rule 4(a) (as amended May 21 and July 6, 2026, effective July 1, 2026).","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"78","t":"Motion day","s":"Each circuit court sets regular times and places for hearing motions requiring notice, unless local conditions make that impracticable, and the judge may make orders advancing and conducting actions at any time. The court may by rule or order decide motions without oral hearing on brief written statements.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"79","t":"Books and records kept by the clerk","s":"The clerk keeps a civil docket in the form the supreme court prescribes, with consecutive file numbers and chronological notation of filings, process, orders, verdicts, and judgments, marking jury actions “jury.” The clerk also keeps copies of final judgments, appealable orders, and orders affecting title to or liens on property, indices, trial calendars distinguishing jury and court actions, and other records the supreme court requires.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"80","t":"Stenographic report or transcript as evidence","s":"Subdivisions (a) and (b) are reserved. When stenographically reported testimony from a trial or hearing is admissible at a later trial, it may be proved by a transcript certified by the reporter.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"81","t":"Applicability","s":"The rules do not apply (unless a rule says otherwise) to probate (HRS ch. 560), guardianship (ch. 551), ex parte trustee and guardian accounts (ch. 554), family court, pre-judgment arbitration applications under ch. 658A, habeas corpus (ch. 660), writs to inferior courts under § 603-21.7(b), bond forfeiture under § 709-51, and corporate meeting calls under § 416-81; they apply, except where inconsistent with specific statutes or rules, to land court, eminent domain, partition and quiet title, quo warranto, escheat, forfeiture, commitment, tax collection, administrative enforcement, election, county-officer impeachment, and a list of other statutory proceedings. Rules apply to probate jury trials (demand by motion within the statutory time), land court appeal jury trials, and agency appeals except as Rule 72 provides; HRAP Rule 4(a) governs appeals from listed proceedings (other than family court) and from land court, discovery rules apply to listed proceedings unless the court finds them infeasible, and the court may order any rule applied. Otherwise the rules govern all civil proceedings in circuit court and civil appeals from circuit court, and “incompetent person” includes anyone other than an infant for whom a guardian may be appointed by statute.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"81.1","t":"Mandamus","s":"The writ of mandamus is abolished in the circuit courts except when directed to a court of inferior jurisdiction; equivalent relief is obtained by appropriate action or motion, and the court may shorten time limits in mandamus-type actions.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","s":"The rules do not extend or limit the circuit courts’ jurisdiction or venue.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"83","t":"Rules by circuit courts","s":"Each circuit court may recommend rules consistent with these rules, which take effect when promulgated by the supreme court and are made available to every licensed attorney; in matters not covered by rule, circuit courts may regulate practice consistently with these rules. This differs from the federal rule, under which district courts adopt local rules themselves.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"84","t":"Forms","s":"The forms in the Appendix of Forms (Forms 1 through 36, including the civil information sheets, publication summons, limited-appearance forms, and scheduling-conference forms) are sufficient under the rules and illustrate the intended simplicity and brevity. Unlike federal Rule 84, which was abrogated in 2015, the Hawai‘i rule and its forms remain in effect.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"85","t":"Title","s":"The rules may be cited as the Hawai‘i Rules of Civil Procedure.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"},{"n":"86","t":"Reserved","s":"Deleted April 7, 1980, effective April 7, 1980.","u":"https://www.courts.state.hi.us/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/hrcp_ada.pdf"}]});
